  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 PM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nMidday surface observations depicted widespread stratus across\nall of SW KS, with elevated southeast winds maintaining moisture\nadvection. Temperatures will continue to struggle in the upper\n50s and lower 60s through 4 pm, with SW KS remaining dry through\n6 pm. \n\nStratus will continue through tonight. A strong low level jet\nwill establish through the night, averaging near 60 mph at \n2k feet. Strong moisture advection, mixing and moisture \nadvection will hold temperatures steady within a few degrees of \n60 through sunrise. This is about 25 degrees above normal for\nearly November. \n\nNumerous showers and thunderstorms are expected to develop \nafter 6 pm across Colorado and New Mexico, in response to the\nfirst strong wave of forcing for ascent. 12z ARW offers a good\nsolution, showing linear convection reaching near the Colorado\nborder about midnight, then reaching the US 283 corridor\n(central CWA) about 3 am Monday. With a strong low level jet and\nrelated strong deep layer shear, and adequate CAPE to near \n1000 J/kg, some strong to severe storms are expected overnight.\nPrimary risk will be wind gusts of 50-60 mph, as the low level\njet momentum is mixed to the ground by thunderstorm downdrafts.\nGiven a saturated boundary layer and low LCLs, a weak tornado or\ntwo, probably embedded within any QLCS, is plausible, but\ncertainly not likely or expected. Thunderstorms are expected to\nexit the southeast zones quickly Monday morning. \n\nPrimary trough axis near the Four Corners 6 am Monday will\nremain quite progressive, near Elkhart 6 pm Monday. The\ncyclone's dry slot will control most of SW KS's weather Monday,\nwith a clearing sky, strong southwest winds, and as such, much\nwarmer afternoon temperatures in the 70s. Most of SW KS will be\ndry for most of daylight Monday. A severe risk remains apparent\nnear Barber County for a few hours Monday afternoon/evening,\nwhere storms can recover from morning convection ahead of the\nprogressive triple point. Any severe threat across/near Barber\ncounty will be brief, before being pushed eastward quickly by\nthe progressive trough. \n\nSurface and midlevel cyclones will exit near Barber county\nMonday evening, allow a strong cold front to race southward\nthrough SW KS with intense north winds - Monday night through\nTuesday morning. Despite the nocturnal timing working against\nmixing, northwest winds will be intense through sunrise Tuesday,\neasily gusting to at least 50 mph. NBM wind grids are about \n5-7 kts weaker than the preferred 12z MAV guidance, especially\nalong the preferred US 83 corridor. High wind warnings may be\nrequired. \n\nSubsidence will prevail Tuesday behind the exiting trough, with\na clearing sky, diminishing northwest winds, and cooler\nafternoon temperatures in the 50s. Much cooler air, much more\ntypical for November, will arrive Wednesday morning, in the mid\nto upper 30s. Some areas of frost are likely in the coldest\nnorthwest zones, but incoming high pressure continues to look \nweak and will be of Pacific origin, continuing to work against \na hard/killing freeze. \n\nAnother small weak shortwave is forecast to arrive Wednesday and\nThursday. This system will be moisture starved, nonetheless some\nscattered rain showers are expected with the passing of this\nsystem midweek. No impacts expected. \n\nAfter a pleasant fall Friday, a stronger, but dry, cold front\npassage is expected sometime next Saturday. This cooler air mass\nmay be cold enough to deliver another freeze next Monday morning\nNovember 9th.",
